lesson "Factorials" {
  course: "pure-maths"
  unit: "Counting & Pascal's Triangle"
  difficulty: "Beginner"
  icon: "Star"
  summary: "How many ways can you arrange things in a row? The answer explodes faster than almost anything in mathematics, and it explains why 0! has to be 1."
  skills: ["comb-factorial", "comb-arrangements", "comb-zero-factorial"]

  slide "Counting the Orders" {
    id: "c1-hook"
    cat: "Arrangements"
    > Three books on a shelf. How many different orders can you put them in?
    >
    > Think of it as filling three slots. The first slot has $3$ candidates. Once
    > that book is placed, the second slot has only $2$ left. The last slot has no
    > choice at all, $1$.
    >
    > $$3 \times 2 \times 1 = 6$$
    >
    > That pattern — count down to one, multiplying — is so common it gets its own
    > symbol:
    >
    > $$n! = n \times (n-1) \times (n-2) \times \cdots \times 2 \times 1$$
    >
    > Add books below and watch the number of possible shelves.

    scene plane {
      x: [-1.0, 11.0]
      y: [-2.6, 4.4]
      aspect: equal
      axes: false
      param n = 4 { range: [1, 8], step: 1 }
      line shelf = (-0.6, 0) -> (10.6, 0) { color: neutral, width: 4 }
      for i in range(0, 8) {
        rect b = (0.2 + i*1.28, 0) { w: 0.95, h: 1.9, color: primary, opacity: 0.4, show: n > i }
      }
      label at (5.0, 3.6) = "${n} books" { anchor: middle, color: primary }
      label at (5.0, -1.4) = "can be arranged in ${fact(n)} different orders" { anchor: middle, color: success }
      slider n { label: "how many books" }
    }

    goal "Get past a thousand possible orders" {
      when: fact(n) > 1000
      hint: "Seven books is already more than five thousand."
    }
  }

  slide "It Grows Absurdly Fast" {
    id: "c1-growth"
    cat: "Arrangements"
    > Factorials outrun everything. Not exponentials, not squares — everything.
    >
    > $5! = 120$. $10!$ is already over three and a half million. $20!$ is bigger
    > than the number of seconds since the dinosaurs. And $52!$, the number of ways
    > to shuffle a deck of cards, is around $8 \times 10^{67}$ — so a genuinely
    > shuffled deck has almost certainly never existed in that order before, in the
    > whole history of card games.
    >
    > This matters practically. It is why brute-force checking every arrangement
    > stops being possible almost immediately.

    table {
      ask "Work out each factorial."
      skill: "comb-factorial"
      header: ["n", "n!"]
      row: 3, blank(6)
      row: 4, blank(24)
      row: 5, blank(120)
      row: 6, blank(720)
      hint "Each one is the previous answer multiplied by the new $n$."
      ! "$6, 24, 120, 720$. Notice you never restart: $6! = 6 \times 5!$. That recursive step is the fastest way to compute them and the reason for the next slide."
    }
  }

  slide "Why 0! = 1" {
    id: "c1-zero"
    cat: "The Odd One"
    > $0! = 1$ looks like a typo. It is forced, by exactly the argument that made
    > $a^0 = 1$.
    >
    > Every factorial obeys $n! = n \times (n-1)!$. Rearranged:
    >
    > $$(n-1)! = \frac{n!}{n}$$
    >
    > Put $n = 1$:
    >
    > $$0! = \frac{1!}{1} = \frac{1}{1} = 1$$
    >
    > There is a second argument, and it is the more satisfying one. $n!$ counts the
    > arrangements of $n$ things. How many ways are there to arrange nothing at all?
    > Exactly one: the empty arrangement. You do not have zero options, you have one
    > option, and it is to do nothing.

    quiz {
      ask "Why is $0!$ defined as $1$ rather than $0$?"
      skill: "comb-zero-factorial"
      * "Because $n! = n \times (n-1)!$ forces it, and there is exactly one way to arrange nothing"
      - "Because multiplying by nothing gives $1$" { why: "Multiplying by nothing is not a defined operation. The reason comes from the recursion $0! = 1!/1$, which pins the value down." }
      - "It is an arbitrary convention" { why: "It is a definition, but the only one that keeps the factorial recursion and every counting formula consistent. Choose $0$ and $nCr$ formulas start dividing by zero." }
      hint "Set $n = 1$ in $(n-1)! = n!/n$ and see what falls out."
      ! "Forced by the recursion, and it matches the counting: there is one way to arrange an empty shelf. Define it as $0$ and every combination formula breaks."
    }
  }

  slide "Arranging Some of Them" {
    id: "c1-partial"
    cat: "The Odd One"
    > Often you do not want to arrange everything. Out of $8$ runners, how many
    > different ways can the gold, silver and bronze be handed out?
    >
    > Same slot-filling logic, but you stop early. $8$ choices for gold, $7$ for
    > silver, $6$ for bronze:
    >
    > $$8 \times 7 \times 6 = 336$$
    >
    > Written with factorials, that is $\dfrac{8!}{5!}$ — the full countdown with the
    > unwanted tail divided off. This is about to be given a name in the next
    > lesson.

    numeric {
      ask "From $6$ people, how many ways can you pick a president and a vice-president, where the two roles are different?"
      skill: "comb-arrangements"
      answer: 30
      expect: 6*5
      tolerance: 0.01
      hint "Six candidates for the first job, then five left for the second."
      ! "$6 \times 5 = 30$. The roles being different is what makes the order matter here: Amira as president with Youssef as deputy is not the same outcome as the reverse."
    }
  }

  slide "Careful With the Symbol" {
    id: "c1-trap"
    cat: "The Odd One"
    quiz {
      ask "Which of these is equal to $\dfrac{7!}{5!}$?"
      skill: "comb-factorial"
      * "$42$"
      - "$\dfrac{7}{5}$, since the factorials cancel" { why: "Factorials do not cancel like that. $7!/5! = (7 \times 6 \times 5!)/5!$, and it is the $5!$ blocks that cancel, leaving $7 \times 6$." }
      - "$2! = 2$" { why: "$7! / 5!$ is not $(7-5)!$. Division of factorials strips off a tail, it does not subtract the numbers." }
      hint "Write $7!$ as $7 \times 6 \times 5!$ and see what cancels."
      ! "$\dfrac{7 \times 6 \times 5!}{5!} = 42$. Writing the bigger factorial in terms of the smaller one is the trick that makes these instant."
    }
  }

  slide "Putting It Together" {
    id: "c1-synth"
    cat: "The Odd One"
    numeric {
      ask "In how many different orders can $5$ people sit in a row of $5$ chairs?"
      skill: "comb-arrangements"
      answer: 120
      expect: fact(5)
      tolerance: 0.01
      hint "All five are being arranged, so it is a straight factorial."
      ! "$5! = 120$. If two of them insisted on sitting together you would have a harder problem, and one you will meet soon enough."
    }
  }
}
